## Mermaid Best Practices

### Styling for Dark Mode

Always use this pattern for visibility in both light and dark themes:

```
classDef className fill:#fff,stroke:#colorCode,stroke-width:2px,color:#000;
```

+ **fill:#fff** — white background (works in both modes)
+ **stroke:#colorCode** — colored border to distinguish node types
+ **color:#000** — black text (legible on white fill)

### Recommended Stroke Colors

| Purpose | Color Code | Visual Effect |
|---------|------------|---------------|
| Start/End nodes | `#2e7d32` | Green — indicates entry/exit |
| Process steps | `#0277bd` | Blue — neutral, main flow |
| Trigger/Alert | `#906` | Purple — draws attention |
| Decision points | `#f57c00` | Orange — indicates branching |
| Loops (dashed) | `#0277bd` with `stroke-dasharray: 5 5` | Dashed blue — cyclical |

### Node Labels

+ Use HTML for rich formatting: `<b>`, `<i>`, `<br/>`
+ Structure: `["<b>Main Label</b><br/><i>Supporting detail</i>"]`
+ Escape quotes inside labels: `&quot;`
+ Keep labels under 20 characters per line for readability

### Graph Sizing

+ Keep node count under **~10 nodes** for clarity
+ If a diagram grows too large, split it into multiple diagrams
+ Use subgraphs to group related nodes visually

