## Example: The Switch Lite Loop

Here's how the post-purchase rationalization loop was visualized:

```mermaid
graph TD
    Buy["<b>Buy the Gadget</b><br/><i>Dopamine spike</i>"]
    Fade["<b>Novelty Fades</b><br/><i>The &quot;new toy&quot; glow wears off</i>"]
    Watch["<b>Watch Reviews</b><br/><i>Re-experience the purchase</i>"]
    Algo["<b>Algorithm Learns</b><br/><i>Serves more of the same</i>"]
    Skip["<b>Break the Loop</b><br/><i>Pick up the device instead</i>"]

    Buy --> Fade
    Fade --> Watch
    Watch --> Algo
    Algo --> Watch
    Watch --> Skip

    classDef trigger fill:#fff,stroke:#906,stroke-width:3px,color:#000;
    classDef loop fill:#fff,stroke:#0277bd,stroke-width:2px,stroke-dasharray: 5 5,color:#000;
    classDef exit fill:#fff,stroke:#2e7d32,stroke-width:2px,color:#000;

    class Buy trigger;
    class Fade,Watch,Algo loop;
    class Skip exit;
```

**Why this works:**
+ Visualizes the cycle clearly (Watch → Algorithm → Watch)
+ Uses color to distinguish trigger (purple), loop (dashed blue), and exit (green)
+ Appears early in the post, giving readers a mental model before the deep-dive
+ Keeps node count manageable (5 nodes)
